  "preamble": "Deaffrication of the voiced postalveolar affricate [dʒ] to the plain stop [d], losing the fricative release while keeping voicing. In Orok (Uilta), a Southern Tungusic language, Proto-Tungusic *ʒ [dʒ] became d in many words (*ʒabda 'big snake' > dabda, *ʒȫr 'two' > дө), while Ulch and Udege keep the affricate; Orok itself keeps ʒ in some words, such as ʒон 'ten' < *ʒuban.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deaffrication, Loss Of Frication",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Orok (Uilta)",
      "languageFamily": "Southern Tungusic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*ʒabda 'big snake'",
          "to": "Orok дабда (dabda)",
          "note": "Ulch дябдян (ʒabʒa(n)), Udege ӡабда (ʒabda) keep the affricate."
        },
        {
          "from": "*ʒapa- 'to grab'",
          "to": "Orok дапавури",
          "note": "Ulch дяпаву (ʒapawu)."
        },
        {
          "from": "*ʒöli 'front'",
          "to": "Orok дули",
          "note": "Ulch дюли (ʒuli)."
        },
        {
          "from": "*ʒȫr 'two'",
          "to": "Orok дө, ду [duː]",
          "note": "Even [dʒɵːr], Evenki [dʒuːr], Ulch [dʒuəl]. Orok keeps the affricate in ʒон (ǯōn) 'ten' < *ʒuban, ӡалан 'generation', ӡоло 'stone'."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
